    "## 7. Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why is ph→my BLEU higher than my→ph BLEU?**\n",
    "Translating phoneme sequences back into Myanmar graphemes (78.15 BLEU) scores higher than the reverse direction (69.38 BLEU). A plausible explanation is that Myanmar orthography draws more fine-grained distinctions than the IPA-style phoneme transcription used here — i.e., several Myanmar spelling variants can collapse onto the same phoneme sequence (historical/etymological spelling, silent letters, tone-marking conventions), making the **my → ph direction the lossier, harder-to-learn mapping**, while ph → my has comparatively less one-to-many ambiguity for the phrase table to resolve.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Main sources of error encountered while reproducing the baseline:**\n",
    "1. Hard-coded absolute paths in `config.baseline`, `generate_configs.pl`, and `run-baseline.pl` (inherited from the instructor's machine) needing per-student path updates.\n",
    "2. A `python` vs `python3` mismatch breaking MGIZA's alignment-merge step.\n",
    "3. An incompatible NIST-BLEU scorer on modern Perl, worked around by switching to `multi-bleu.perl`.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Possible next steps to improve the baseline:**\n",
    "- Increase LM order from the default (`order = 5`, already fairly high) or try interpolating multiple LM orders.\n",
    "- Increase `max-phrase-length` (currently 5) since G2P units are short syllables and longer phrase context might help disambiguate tone/vowel mapping.\n",
    "- Try `wbe-msd-bidirectional-fe` reordering instead of `msd-bidirectional-fe` (reordering matters less for G2P, which is largely monotonic, so gains here are likely small).\n",
    "- Run additional MERT iterations / multiple random restarts for more stable tuning weights.\n",
    "- Apply significance-test phrase-table filtering (`sigtest-filter`) to prune noisy low-confidence phrase pairs from the 20k-sentence training set."
   ]
  },
